@lang en
IMPORT prelude
IMPORT daydate
IMPORT `baominh-par-conditions`
IMPORT `baominh-par-fixtures`

-- Tests for layer 3: the general conditions. Every expected value was decided from the clause
-- cited beside it before the assertion was run; both sides of every period are tested. The
-- short-period scale's rows and their expected percentages are GENERATED from the raw text by
-- gen-scale.py (not retyped). The #TRACE directives print the deontic rules' outcomes; NOTES.md
-- section 6 records them.

§ `Tests: the general conditions`

§§ `General Condition 2`

GIVEN w IS A `What the Insured told the Insurer about the item`
GIVETH A `A damaged item`
`the burnt building, where the Insured told the Insurer` w MEANS `A damaged item` WITH
    `the item insured`                  IS `the building`
    `kind`                              IS buildings
    `the chain of causation`            IS LIST (`directly` fire)
    `circumstances`                     IS `the ordinary circumstances`
    `valuation`                         IS `the ordinary valuation`
    `what the Insured told the Insurer` IS w
    `changes under General Condition 8` IS EMPTY
    `claimed as a cost of changing or adding to a computer system, programme, software or chip` IS FALSE

#ASSERT `why General Condition 2 takes the item out` (`the burnt building, where the Insured told the Insurer` `nothing false was said`) EQUALS EMPTY
#ASSERT `why General Condition 2 takes the item out` (`the burnt building, where the Insured told the Insurer` (`What the Insured told the Insurer about the item` WITH `false information about the property, the building, the business or the premises` IS FALSE, `a misstatement of a material fact` IS TRUE, `an omission of a fact on which the Insurer assessed the risk` IS FALSE)) EQUALS LIST "General Condition 2: the property is affected by a misstatement of a material fact"
#ASSERT `why General Condition 2 takes the item out` (`the burnt building, where the Insured told the Insurer` (`What the Insured told the Insurer about the item` WITH `false information about the property, the building, the business or the premises` IS FALSE, `a misstatement of a material fact` IS FALSE, `an omission of a fact on which the Insurer assessed the risk` IS TRUE)) EQUALS LIST "General Condition 2: the property is affected by an omission of a fact on which the Insurer assessed the risk"

§§ `General Condition 3: the Insurer cancels`

-- sent 30 June 2026: effective 7 July (7 days, fork F19); 177 days of 2026 are left after it
#ASSERT `the day the Insurer's cancellation takes effect, for a letter sent on` (YMD 2026 6 30) EQUALS YMD 2026 7 7
#ASSERT `the number of days in` `the year 2026` EQUALS 365
#ASSERT `the premium refunded when the Insurer cancels by a letter sent on` `the Schedule` (YMD 2026 6 30) EQUALS 12000000 TIMES 177 DIVIDED BY 365
-- the edge: sent 24 December, effective on the last day, no day left; sent 23 December, one day left
#ASSERT `the premium refunded when the Insurer cancels by a letter sent on` `the Schedule` (YMD 2026 12 24) EQUALS 0
#ASSERT `the premium refunded when the Insurer cancels by a letter sent on` `the Schedule` (YMD 2026 12 23) EQUALS 12000000 DIVIDED BY 365
-- sent 28 December: the cancellation would take effect after the period ends; nothing to refund
#ASSERT `the premium refunded when the Insurer cancels by a letter sent on` `the Schedule` (YMD 2026 12 28) EQUALS 0

§§ `General Condition 3: the Insured ends the cover, and the short-period scale`

-- GENERATED by gen-scale.py from src:277-281: expected percentages are read from the raw text.
